# XSH longjmp()
# Copyright (c) 2026 Nicholas Martin. All rights reserved.
#
# Whether longjmp() restores the signal mask is unspecified and is not
# tested here (see siglongjmp for the signal mask).

assertion  XSH.longjmp.001
anchor     functions/longjmp.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/longjmp.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      1988
says       longjmp(env, val) makes the matching setjmp() return val, for
           val of 1, 2, 7, -5, INT_MAX and INT_MIN.
test       t/001-value.c

assertion  XSH.longjmp.002
anchor     functions/longjmp.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      1988
says       longjmp(env, 0) makes setjmp() return 1, never 0.
test       t/002-zero-value.c

assertion  XSH.longjmp.003
anchor     functions/longjmp.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      1988
says       At the jump, volatile locals, globals and objects in memory have
           the values they held when longjmp() was called (changed after
           setjmp()), and the state of an open file (its offset after a
           write between the calls) is as at the call.
test       t/003-state-kept.c

assertion  XSH.longjmp.004
anchor     functions/longjmp.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      1988
says       longjmp() unwinds any number of active calls: from 50 nested
           calls, and repeated 1000 times, the matching setjmp() resumes in
           the function that called it with its volatile locals intact.
test       t/004-nested.c

assertion  XSH.longjmp.005
anchor     functions/longjmp.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/longjmp.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      1988
says       longjmp() may be called from a signal handler that interrupted the
           function with the setjmp(): after raise(), the handler's longjmp()
           returns control there with the value given.
note       longjmp() is async-signal-safe (XSH 2.4.3).
test       t/005-from-handler.c
